00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:42.981
Announcer:
<i> The strange world
 of the computers.</i>

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:44.049
<i> Devised by engineers...</i>

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:45.417
Pre-Apple,
the computer industry

00:04:45.484 --> 00:04:48.587
<i> was really found
 in just a handful of places--</i>

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:51.623
<i> the military,
 scientific institutes.</i>

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.126
Humphrey:
<i> It was something
 that companies did</i>

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:56.795
<i> to crunch massive amounts
 of data.</i>

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.897
The original computers, they
were very cinematic in a way.

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:01.066
<i> They took up an entire room.</i>

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:03.001
<i>They had the giant reel-to-reel
 tape machines on them.</i>

00:05:03.068 --> 00:05:05.471
<i> They whirred and clicked
 and made cool noises.</i>

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:07.072
( imitating whirring )

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:08.607
<i>You had guys in white lab coats</i>

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:09.575
<i> who were the only ones
 who were allowed to use them.</i>

00:05:09.641 --> 00:05:12.378
<i> They didn't even have screens.</i>

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.413
Humphrey:<i> So at that time,</i>

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.749
<i> IBM was the biggest computer
 company in the world.</i>

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:20.686
Segall:<i> IBM-- International
 Business Machines.</i>

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:23.655 align:start position:0%
You couldn't have
a more cold and inhuman name.

00:05:23.722 --> 00:05:25.758
Ackerman:
<i> They'd been around making,
 you know, calculators</i>

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:27.659
<i> and other things for decades.</i>

00:05:27.726 --> 00:05:29.595
<i> They eventually started
 making computers,</i>

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.831
but they had no concept
of the home computer.

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:34.967
<i> How are you gonna take
 an idea like the computer,</i>

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:37.736
<i> this big room-size
 intimidating machine,</i>

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:39.471
<i> and make it
 a mainstream thing?</i>

00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:42.441
Well, the answer
is the microprocessor.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.710
<i> Pioneered in the early '70s,
 it basically said</i>

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.712
<i> we can take the brain
 of the computer</i>

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:48.814
<i> and shrink it down
 to micro-size.</i>

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:52.684
Humphrey:
<i> So that really allows for
 smaller, faster,</i>

00:05:52.751 --> 00:05:54.052
<i> and much cheaper hardware.</i>

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:55.954
<i> And I think Steve Jobs
 was such a visionary</i>

00:05:56.021 --> 00:05:59.024
that he saw where
that could go.

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:02.060 line:0%
<i> Steve Jobs, Steve Wozniak,</i>

00:06:02.127 --> 00:06:05.097 line:0%
Ronald Wayne, they were
computer hobbyists in a club.

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:08.700
Panitz:<i> In its early days,
 Apple was a couple of guys
 in a garage</i>

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:11.703
just kicking around ideas and
putting together some, frankly,

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.305
pretty janky-looking
prototypes.

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:15.908
Humphrey:<i> The Apple I
 was their first computer,</i>

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:18.877 line:0%
<i> and it literally was just
 a circuit board.</i>

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:22.681
Panitz:
<i> The Apple I looks like a bad
 woodshop class in high school.</i>

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:24.149
